2610.34036v1 | A simple construction of smooth counterexamples to the Loewner and Carathéodory conjectures | Naoya Ando, Masaaki Umehara 
      Alpöge recently announced smooth counterexamples to the Loewner and Carathéodory conjectures. We present a simpler construction based on a trigonometric function as a seed. Using this, for every positive integer $k$, we construct a smooth real-valued function on the plane, flat at the origin, whose Hessian eigenline fields have an isolated singularity of index $1+k/2$. A support-function parametrization realizes this index as an umbilic index of a non-real analytic $C^\infty$-regular surface germ in Euclidean $3$-space. The same framework, applied to a rapidly decreasing function on the whole plane, gives a strictly convex closed smooth surface with exactly one umbilic, of index $2$. This pa
2402.17060v2 | Degenerate Umbilic Points of Analytic Surfaces | John Guckenheimaer 
      Umbilics are points of a surface embedded in three space where normal curvatures are independent of direction. The (in)famous Carathéodory Conjecture states that a compact simply connected embedded surface has at least two umbilic points. A counterexample to this conjecture would be a surface whose principal foliation has index two at a single umbilic. All (purported) proofs of the Carathéodory Conjecture are based on analyses of the index of an umbilic, concluding that it is at most one. This investigation gives a much simpler geometric argument that the index of an umbilic on an analytic surface cannot be an integer larger than one, providing new insight into the Carathéodory Conjecture. T
